  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Fri May 8 2026\n\nSaturday evening, will keep some low end slight chance PoPs going\nfor Haskell and Throckmorton counties into the evening hours as \nnumerous high resolution outputs show a an isolated storm or two \naffecting our northern two counties. There is also a Marginal Risk\nfor severe storms Saturday afternoon and evening from the SPC, \nalthough the risk will be limited in intensity and coverage. \n\nOn Sunday afternoon/evening, a shortwave trough will move \nsoutheast through the southern plains, likely making it to the \ndeep south and Texas gulf coast by early Monday morning. \nAccompanying this upper level trough will be a fast-moving cold \nfront. Warm unstable air ahead of this cold front will increase \ninstability (SB CAPE values nearing 3000 J/kg), along with mid-\nlevel lapse rates of 7.5 to 8 C/km. The combination of these \nfeatures is expected to result in a line of thunderstorms \ndeveloping by mid to late afternoon Sunday, from west-central \nTexas east into North Texas. Although deep layer shear won't be \nexceptionally high, there will be enough when combined with the \ninstability to result in some of these storms becoming strong to \nsevere as they move southeast across our area. The main concerns \nwill be large hail and damaging winds, although an isolated \ntornado or two can't be ruled out. Expect the line/cluster of \nstorms to move through rather quickly, and likely be out of our \narea for the most part by mid to late evening. \n\nNorthwest flow on Monday will be gradually replaced by upper \nlevel ridging through late next week, with generally dry weather \nexpected. Following the cold front Sunday night, expect cooler \nthan normal temperatures for Monday, then temperatures getting \nwarmer than normal by the middle of next week.",
